WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.440
No, the father of the atomic bomb doesn't&nbsp;
pull off any heists inside dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.186
but there are some clear similarities&nbsp;
between "Oppenheimer" and "Inception."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.186 --> 00:00:10.932
Director Christopher Nolan himself says so.

00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:16.500
Audiences are gearing up to see "Oppenheimer,"&nbsp;
Nolan's much-hyped summer 2023 release. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.740
in an interview with Wired, Nolan&nbsp;
himself seems to be looking to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.317
past a bit, particularly toward his&nbsp;
mind-bending 2010 film "Inception."

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:29.350
The final sequence from "Inception" is always&nbsp;
a fun discussion point among film enthusiasts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.860
The film's enigmatic closing moments inspire&nbsp;
multiple interpretations. Did Leonardo DiCaprio's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:40.080
Cobb truly resurface from the dream world and find&nbsp;
his happily ever after with his kids? Or will the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.366
dream totem continue spinning forever and signal&nbsp;
that he's really still submerged inside a dream?

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:49.476
"When you look at your totem, you know beyond a&nbsp;
doubt that you're not in someone else's dream."

00:00:49.476 --> 00:00:53.100
Does Cobb even care after all he's&nbsp;
been through? Nolan told Wired,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.080
"I mean, the end of 'Inception,' it's exactly&nbsp;
that. There is a nihilistic view of that ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.160
right? But also, he's moved on and&nbsp;
is with his kids. The ambiguity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.901
not an emotional ambiguity. It's an&nbsp;
intellectual one for the audience."

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:09.480
The ending of "Oppenheimer" is less&nbsp;
ambiguous, but it's still an unsettling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.920
finale. Nolan himself noted the similarities&nbsp;
to the conclusion of "Inception," saying,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.820
"It's funny, I think there is an interesting&nbsp;
relationship between the endings of 'Inception'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.344
and 'Oppenheimer' to be explored. 'Oppenheimer''s&nbsp;
got a complicated ending. Complicated feelings."

00:01:23.344 --> 00:01:27.960
That complexity is often a trademark of Nolan's&nbsp;
work that threads together his vision from film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.460
to film. Those who have seen "Oppenheimer" have&nbsp;
noticed it in his latest release, which is based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.200
on the startling truths revealed in the book&nbsp;
"American Prometheus: The Triumph and Tragedy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.420
J. Robert Oppenheimer." It captures the harrowing&nbsp;
reality of the creation of the atomic bomb, two of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.580
which were dropped on the Japanese cities Nagasaki&nbsp;
and Hiroshima by U.S. forces during World War II.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.020
Nolan's film explores the ethical boundaries&nbsp;
crossed by the title character, who played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.400
pivotal role in bringing the weapon of mass&nbsp;
destruction to life. J. Robert Oppenheimer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.140
was even dubbed "the father of the atomic bomb"&nbsp;
for his central involvement and research in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.000
initiative to build the a-bomb, the Manhattan&nbsp;
Project. Nolan told Wired of his new film,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.140
"Some people leave the movie absolutely&nbsp;
devastated. They can't speak. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.641
there's an element of fear that's there in&nbsp;
the history and there in the underpinnings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.641 --> 00:02:18.222
But the love of the characters, the love of the&nbsp;
relationships, is as strong as I've ever done."

00:02:18.222 --> 00:02:20.100
That strong character development would seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.880
strengthen the comparison to "Inception,"&nbsp;
along with the fact that both films have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.600
Cillian Murphy in the cast. He plays&nbsp;
the title character in "Oppenheimer."

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.660
Nolan took a complex, fascinating&nbsp;
approach to telling the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.620
"Oppenheimer." Speaking with Total Film,&nbsp;
he shared that he set out to recall the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.340
historical events depicted in the film&nbsp;
through J. Robert Oppenheimer's eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.840
making the otherwise straightforward&nbsp;
historical drama more personal. Nolan said,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.140
"We're trying to tell the story of somebody's&nbsp;
life and their journey through personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.520
history and larger-scale history, and so the&nbsp;
subjectivity of the story is everything to me."

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.640
“In the case of this film, I wrote this script in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.122
the first person. It’s the&nbsp;
only time I’ve done that.”

00:02:56.122 --> 00:03:00.653
Nolan's character-centric view definitely&nbsp;
had an effect on Murphy. The director added,

00:03:00.653 --> 00:03:04.260
"We want to view these events through&nbsp;
Oppenheimer's eyes. And that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.478
challenge for Cillian that I set&nbsp;
him, to take us on this journey."

00:03:07.478 --> 00:03:10.980
Nolan noted that Murphy's challenge&nbsp;
extended to pretty much the entire team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.000
behind the movie. Every choice made had&nbsp;
to somehow circle back to Oppenheimer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.840
perspective on the world during such a&nbsp;
turbulent, yet pivotal point in history.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.340
"Oppenheimer" opens on July 21.

